## DESCRIPTION
##   Equation of the Line of Intersection
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## KEYWORDS('Intersect', 'Unit', 'Parallel', 'Vector Equation', 'Line', 'Plane')
## DBsubject('Calculus')
## DBchapter('Vectors and the Geometry of Space')
## DBsection('Equations of Lines and Planes')
## Date('2/2/2014')
## Author('Darwyn Cook')
## Institution('Alfred University')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Point");

$a = random(1, 5);
$b = random(1, 5);
$c = random(1, 5);
$P = Point("(0,1/$b,0)");

Context("Vector");
Context()->variables->are(t=>"Real");

$n1 = Vector($a,$b,$c);
$n2 = Vector($a,0,$c);
$parallelvector = $n1 x $n2;

$r = Formula("<0,1/$b,0>+t*$parallelvector");

BEGIN_PGML
Consider the planes 
[` [$a]x + [$b]y + [$c]z = 1 `] and 
[` [$a]x + [$c]z = 0. `]

a) Find the unique point [`P`] on the [`y`]-axis which is on both planes. [_]{$P}

b) Find a vector normal to [` [$a]x + [$b]y + [$c]z = 1 `]: [_]{$n1}

c) Find a vector normal to [` [$a]x + [$c]z = 0 `]: [_]{$n2}

d) Find a vector parallel to both planes: [_]{$parallelvector->cmp(parallel => 1)}

e) Find a parametric equation for the line of intersection of the two planes: [_]{$r}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) If it is on the [`y`]-axis then both [`x=0`] and [`z=0`] and using the first equation [`x=[$P]`].

b) A normal vector to the plane [` [$a]x + [$b]y + [$c]z = 1 `] is the coefficients of the [`x,y`] and [`z`] or [`[$n1]`].

c) A normal vector to the plane [` [$a]x + [$c]z = 0 `] is the coefficients of the [`x,y`] and [`z`] or [`[$n2]`].

d) A vector parallel to both planes is found with 
[```
\vec{n}_1 \times \vec{n}_2 = [$parallelvector]
```]
and any scale multiple of this is also allowed.

e) This is a line that passes through the point [`[$P]`] and parallel to [`[$parallelvector]`] is  
[```\vec{r} = [$r].```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();